# Normalise line endings in the repository.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks files out as CRLF and stages them back as
# CRLF, so files that were committed with LF show up as wholly rewritten the first time anyone
# touches them. That happened here: a single docs pass produced ~17 "modified" files whose content
# had not changed at all, which inflates diffs, obscures the real edits in review, and makes
# `git blame` point at the line-ending commit instead of the author of the text.
#
# `text=auto` stores everything Git detects as text with LF in the object database while leaving
# working-tree endings to the platform, so this is invisible day to day.
* text=auto

# Keep the shells' scripts LF regardless of platform — Git Bash and CI both run them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell is Windows-native tooling; CRLF in the working tree is what its editors expect.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch these. Model weights and compiled shaders are large and byte-exact —
# an accidental line-ending rewrite would corrupt them silently.
*.gguf     binary
*.safetensors binary
*.spv      binary
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.ico      binary
*.snk      binary
*.nupkg    binary
*.snupkg   binary
*.dll      binary
*.exe      binary
*.so       binary
*.dylib    binary
*.lib      binary
*.pdb      binary
